        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/adc92151/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator/requirements.md",
        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-02-01\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA simple YAML syntax validation command integrated into the rp1 CLI that allows users to quickly verify whether one or more YAML files are syntactically valid, providing clear human-readable feedback without requiring any additional dependencies.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration files need a quick and reliable way to validate file syntax before using them in workflows, deployments, or other processes. Invalid YAML can cause failures downstream, and early detection saves time and prevents errors.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces debugging time by catching YAML syntax errors early\n- Improves developer productivity with immediate feedback\n- Maintains a lightweight tool footprint by not adding external dependencies\n- Provides a consistent validation experience within the rp1 toolchain\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Valid file correctly identified | 100% accuracy |\n| Invalid file correctly identified | 100% accuracy with line number reporting |\n| Command execution time | < 1 second for typical files |\n| User adoption | Integrated into developer workflows |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Need |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|\n| Developer | Uses rp1 CLI for development workflows | Quick syntax validation of YAML config files |\n| DevOps Engineer | Manages configuration and deployment files | Validate YAML before deployment |\n| CI/CD Pipeline | Automated build and deploy processes | Programmatic validation with clear exit codes |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Maintain clean, valid configuration files |\n| Operations Team | Prevent deployment failures from invalid configs |\n| Product Team | Reliable tooling that enhances productivity |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Syntax validation of YAML files\n- Accepting one or more file paths as command arguments\n- Human-readable output messages indicating validity or error details\n- Line number reporting for syntax errors\n- Integration as an rp1 subcommand (`rp1 validate-yaml`)\n- Appropriate exit codes for scripting (0 = success, non-zero = failure)\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating against a specific structure)\n- Reading from stdin\n- Directory scanning or glob pattern support\n- JSON or machine-readable output formats\n- Auto-fix or formatting capabilities\n- Support for remote file URLs\n- Adding new external dependencies\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The rp1 CLI framework already exists and supports adding subcommands\n- The runtime environment has built-in YAML parsing capabilities (no new deps needed)\n- Users have local file system access to the YAML files they want to validate\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: Single File Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer, DevOps Engineer |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall accept a single file path as an argument and validate its YAML syntax |\n| **Rationale** | Core functionality - users need to validate individual files |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given a valid YAML file path, when the command is executed, then the file is parsed and validity is reported |\n\n### REQ-002: Multiple File Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Developer, DevOps Engineer |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall accept multiple file paths as arguments and validate each file's YAML syntax |\n| **Rationale** | Users often need to validate multiple configuration files at once |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given multiple file paths, when the command is executed, then each file is validated and results are reported for all files |\n\n### REQ-003: Valid File Feedback\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | All Users |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall display a clear \"valid\" message for each syntactically correct YAML file |\n| **Rationale** | Users need positive confirmation that their files are correct |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given a valid YAML file, when validated, then a message indicating the file is valid is displayed |\n\n### REQ-004: Invalid File Error Reporting\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | All Users |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall display an error message with line number information for syntactically invalid YAML files |\n| **Rationale** | Users need to know where errors occur to fix them efficiently |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given an invalid YAML file with an error on line 5, when validated, then a message like \"error on line 5\" is displayed |\n\n### REQ-005: File Not Found Handling\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | All Users |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall display an appropriate error message when a specified file does not exist |\n| **Rationale** | Users need clear feedback when file paths are incorrect |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given a non-existent file path, when the command is executed, then a \"file not found\" error message is displayed |\n\n### REQ-006: Exit Code for Success\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall return exit code 0 when all provided files are valid |\n| **Rationale** | Enables use in scripts and CI/CD pipelines |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given all valid files, when the command completes, then exit code is 0 |\n\n### REQ-007: Exit Code for Failure\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **Requirement** | The system shall return a non-zero exit code when any provided file is invalid or cannot be read |\n| **Rationale** | Enables use in scripts and CI/CD pipelines to detect failures |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given at least one invalid file, when the command completes, then exit code is non-zero |\n\n### REQ-008: Subcommand Integration\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | All Users |\n| **Requirement** | The validator shall be accessible as the `rp1 validate-yaml` subcommand |\n| **Rationale** | Consistent integration with existing rp1 CLI tooling |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given rp1 CLI is installed, when user runs `rp1 validate-yaml <file>`, then the validation is performed |\n\n### REQ-009: No New Dependencies\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Development Team |\n| **Requirement** | The implementation shall not introduce any new external dependencies to the project |\n| **Rationale** | Maintain lightweight footprint and reduce dependency management burden |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | Given the feature is implemented, when dependencies are audited, then no new packages have been added |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Requirement | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Single file validation | < 500ms for files up to 1MB |\n| Multiple file validation | Linear scaling with file count |\n| Memory usage | Reasonable for typical YAML files (< 100MB) |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File access | Only read files explicitly specified by the user |\n| No code execution | Parser must not execute any code embedded in YAML |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Clear messages | Output must be easily understood by developers |\n| Consistent format | Error messages follow a predictable pattern |\n| Help text | Command includes `--help` with usage examples |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNone identified for this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate Single Configuration File\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | Developer |\n| **I want** | to validate a single YAML configuration file |\n| **So that** | I can ensure it has no syntax errors before using it |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a YAML file path\n- WHEN I run `rp1 validate-yaml config.yaml`\n- THEN I see either \"valid\" or an error message with line number\n\n### STORY-002: Validate Multiple Files at Once\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | DevOps Engineer |\n| **I want** | to validate multiple YAML files in a single command |\n| **So that** | I can quickly check all my configuration files |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN multiple YAML file paths\n- WHEN I run `rp1 validate-yaml file1.yaml file2.yaml file3.yaml`\n- THEN I see validation results for each file\n\n### STORY-003: Use in CI Pipeline\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | CI/CD Pipeline |\n| **I want** | the command to return appropriate exit codes |\n| **So that** | I can fail the build when YAML files are invalid |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a CI pipeline step running `rp1 validate-yaml *.yaml`\n- WHEN any file is invalid\n- THEN the command returns non-zero exit code and pipeline fails\n\n### STORY-004: Locate Syntax Errors\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **As a** | Developer |\n| **I want** | to see the line number where a YAML error occurs |\n| **So that** | I can quickly navigate to and fix the problem |\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error on line 15\n- WHEN I run `rp1 validate-yaml broken.yaml`\n- THEN I see an error message indicating line 15\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule |\n|---------|------|\n| BR-001 | All specified files must be validated, even if earlier files have errors |\n| BR-002 | Exit code reflects overall result (0 only if ALL files valid) |\n| BR-003 | File paths are interpreted relative to current working directory |\n| BR-004 | Empty files are considered valid YAML (YAML spec allows empty documents) |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| rp1 CLI framework | Internal | Existing CLI structure for adding subcommands |\n| Built-in YAML parser | Internal | Runtime's native YAML parsing capability |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| No new dependencies | Must use only existing project dependencies |\n| Syntax validation only | No schema or semantic validation |\n| Local files only | No remote URL or stdin support |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Answer | Date |\n|----------|--------|------|\n| Who will use this command? | Developers, DevOps Engineers, CI/CD pipelines | 2026-02-01 |\n| What input methods? | File path(s) only - one or more as arguments | 2026-02-01 |\n| What output format? | Human-readable messages (e.g., \"valid\" or \"error on line 5\") | 2026-02-01 |\n| What validation level? | Syntax only - check if YAML is syntactically valid | 2026-02-01 |\n| Standalone or integrated? | rp1 subcommand - add as `rp1 validate-yaml` | 2026-02-01 |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
