        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Created**: 2026-02-01\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | CLI command structure | Commands directory pattern with dispatcher | User preference; provides extensibility for future commands; clean separation of concerns | Simple switch statement in index.ts (less extensible) |\n| D2 | YAML parsing library | js-yaml via Bun built-in | Bun bundles js-yaml internally, allowing import without adding to package.json; meets no-new-deps requirement | Manual regex-based validation (unreliable), native Bun API (not available for YAML) |\n| D3 | Output format | Human-readable text only | Requirements explicitly specify human-readable output; JSON/machine-readable formats are out of scope | JSON output format (explicitly out of scope) |\n| D4 | Error line number indexing | 1-indexed (starting from line 1) | Standard convention matching editors, IDEs, and user expectations; js-yaml uses 0-indexed internally so conversion needed | 0-indexed (confusing for users) |\n| D5 | Multiple file validation behavior | Validate all files regardless of earlier errors | Business rule BR-001 requires validating all specified files; users need complete picture of all errors | Fail-fast on first error (violates requirements) |\n| D6 | Exit code strategy | Exit 0 only when ALL files valid; Exit 1 otherwise | Business rule BR-002; standard CI/CD convention for validation tools | Different exit codes for different error types (unnecessarily complex) |\n| D7 | File path resolution | Relative to current working directory | Business rule BR-003; standard CLI behavior; matches user expectations | Relative to script location (non-standard) |\n| D8 | Empty file handling | Treat as valid YAML | Business rule BR-004; YAML specification allows empty documents | Treat as error (violates YAML spec) |\n| D9 | Command interface design | Async execute() returning { exitCode } | Allows for future async operations; clean contract for all commands | Sync execution (limits future flexibility) |\n| D10 | Test file organization | Co-located .test.ts files + fixtures directory | Matches existing project pattern (utils.test.ts); fixtures provide reusable test data | Separate test directory (deviates from existing pattern) |\n\n## Technology Selection Rationale\n\n### YAML Parser Selection\n\n**Requirement**: Parse YAML files and report syntax errors with line numbers, without adding new dependencies.\n\n**Analysis**:\n- Bun runtime bundles several npm packages internally\n- `js-yaml` is available as a Bun built-in module\n- Importing `js-yaml` in Bun does not require adding to package.json\n- `js-yaml` provides `YAMLException` with `mark` property containing line/column info\n\n**Risk**: If Bun changes its bundled packages in future versions, this could break. Mitigation: Pin Bun version or add js-yaml as explicit dependency if needed.\n\n### CLI Structure Selection\n\n**Requirement**: Integrate as `rp1 validate-yaml` subcommand with extensibility for future commands.\n\n**User Choice**: Commands directory pattern\n\n**Benefits**:\n- Each command is a self-contained module\n- Easy to add new commands without modifying dispatcher\n- Commands can be tested in isolation\n- Clear ownership and discoverability\n\n**Trade-offs**:\n- Slightly more boilerplate than simple switch statement\n- Requires defining Command interface\n\n## Security Considerations\n\n| Concern | Decision | Rationale |\n|---------|----------|-----------|\n| YAML code execution | Use yaml.load() with default safe options | js-yaml safe mode prevents code execution via YAML tags |\n| File access | Only read explicitly specified files | No directory traversal, glob expansion, or implicit file discovery |\n| Path traversal | Use standard path resolution | Rely on OS/Bun file system protections |\n\n## Performance Considerations\n\n| Aspect | Decision | Rationale |\n|--------|----------|-----------|\n| File reading | Read entire file into memory | Acceptable for typical YAML configs; requirement allows < 100MB |\n| Sequential processing | Process files sequentially | Simpler implementation; parallel processing not needed for typical use case |\n| No caching | Parse each file fresh | Validation is typically a one-shot operation |\n"
