        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-02-01\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line utility that validates YAML files for syntax correctness. The validator will be implemented without introducing any new external dependencies, leveraging only the existing project infrastructure and built-in capabilities.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files need a quick way to validate file syntax before deployment or use. Currently, there is no built-in validation capability in the project, requiring developers to use external tools or discover syntax errors only at runtime.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging YAML syntax errors\n- Catches configuration errors early in the development workflow\n- Maintains a lean dependency footprint by avoiding new package installations\n- Integrates seamlessly with existing project tooling\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Validation accuracy | 100% detection of invalid YAML syntax |\n| Command execution time | Under 500ms for typical config files |\n| Zero new dependencies | Confirmed via package.json review |\n| User adoption | Command available and documented |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Need |\n|-----------|-------------|--------------|\n| Developer | Project contributor working with YAML configs | Quick validation during development |\n| CI System | Automated build/deploy pipeline | Pre-deployment validation checks |\n| DevOps Engineer | Infrastructure/deployment specialist | Configuration file verification |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Maintain simple, dependency-light codebase |\n| Project Maintainers | Consistent tooling that follows project patterns |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- Command-line interface for YAML validation\n- Validation of single YAML files\n- Clear error messages indicating validation failures with line/position info where possible\n- Exit codes suitable for CI/CD integration (0 for valid, non-zero for invalid)\n- Support for standard YAML syntax\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating YAML content against a schema)\n- YAML formatting or auto-fixing\n- Batch validation of multiple files in a single command\n- YAML to JSON conversion\n- Adding any new npm dependencies\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Bun runtime provides native YAML parsing capability or an alternative approach exists\n- Files to validate are accessible on the local filesystem\n- Input files use UTF-8 encoding\n- Command will be invoked via the existing project script infrastructure\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: File Input Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer, CI System |\n| Requirement | The validator shall accept a file path as a command-line argument and verify the file exists before attempting validation |\n| Rationale | Users need to specify which file to validate; early existence check prevents confusing errors |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Given a valid file path, the validator reads the file. Given an invalid path, the validator outputs a clear \"file not found\" error and exits with non-zero code |\n\n### REQ-002: YAML Syntax Validation\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer, CI System, DevOps Engineer |\n| Requirement | The validator shall parse the YAML content and detect syntax errors including malformed indentation, invalid characters, and structural issues |\n| Rationale | Core functionality - detecting invalid YAML is the primary purpose |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Valid YAML files pass validation. Invalid YAML files produce error output describing the issue |\n\n### REQ-003: Success Output\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | Upon successful validation, the validator shall output a confirmation message and exit with code 0 |\n| Rationale | Users need clear feedback that validation passed |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Output includes filename and \"valid\" status. Exit code is 0 |\n\n### REQ-004: Error Output\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Developer, DevOps Engineer |\n| Requirement | Upon validation failure, the validator shall output an error message including the filename, error type, and location information when available |\n| Rationale | Developers need actionable information to fix YAML issues |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Error output includes filename, error description, and line number if available. Exit code is non-zero |\n\n### REQ-005: No New Dependencies\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Must Have |\n| User Type | Project Maintainers |\n| Requirement | The validator shall be implemented using only existing project dependencies and built-in runtime capabilities |\n| Rationale | Explicit requirement to maintain lean dependency footprint |\n| Acceptance Criteria | package.json dependencies remain unchanged after implementation |\n\n### REQ-006: Script Integration\n| Attribute | Value |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Priority | Should Have |\n| User Type | Developer |\n| Requirement | The validator command should be accessible via a package.json script entry |\n| Rationale | Consistent with existing project patterns (dev, test, lint, format scripts) |\n| Acceptance Criteria | Validator can be invoked via `bun run validate-yaml <file>` or similar |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Requirement | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation time for files under 1MB | Under 500ms |\n| Memory usage | Proportional to file size, no excessive allocation |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File access | Only read access required; no file modification |\n| Path traversal | Accept only explicit file paths; no glob expansion required |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Error clarity | Error messages must be understandable without consulting documentation |\n| Help text | Basic usage information when invoked without arguments |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNo specific compliance requirements identified.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Developer Validates Config File\n\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to validate a YAML configuration file from the command line\n**So that** I can catch syntax errors before using the file\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a valid YAML file path\n- WHEN I run the validate command with that path\n- THEN I see a success message and the command exits with code 0\n\n- GIVEN an invalid YAML file path\n- WHEN I run the validate command with that path\n- THEN I see an error message with details and the command exits with non-zero code\n\n### STORY-002: CI Pipeline Validates Configs\n\n**As a** CI system\n**I want** to run YAML validation as a build step\n**So that** invalid configurations fail the build early\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN a YAML validation step in CI\n- WHEN the file is valid\n- THEN the step passes (exit code 0)\n\n- GIVEN a YAML validation step in CI\n- WHEN the file is invalid\n- THEN the step fails (non-zero exit code) with descriptive output\n\n### STORY-003: Developer Gets Help\n\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to see usage instructions when I run the command incorrectly\n**So that** I know how to use the validator properly\n\n**Acceptance Criteria:**\n- GIVEN no arguments provided\n- WHEN I run the validate command\n- THEN I see usage instructions\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule |\n|---------|------|\n| BR-001 | Empty files shall be considered valid YAML (empty document) |\n| BR-002 | Files with multiple YAML documents (separated by `---`) shall be validated as a whole |\n| BR-003 | The validator shall not modify any files |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Bun runtime | Technical | Required for execution |\n| Existing project structure | Technical | Must integrate with current src/ layout |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| No new npm packages | Must use only existing dependencies and built-in capabilities |\n| TypeScript | Implementation should follow existing TypeScript patterns |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Resolution | Source |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| What YAML parser to use? | Use Bun/Node built-in capabilities or implement minimal parser | Inferred from no-deps requirement |\n| Multiple file support? | Out of scope for initial version | Conservative default |\n| Schema validation? | Out of scope - syntax validation only | Inferred from \"simple\" requirement |\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None | No PRD files found in .rp1/work/prds/ |\n| KB context | Best-effort inference | KB files (index.md, concept_map.md) do not exist |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" - scope unclear | Syntax validation only, no schema validation | Conservative interpretation of \"simple\" |\n| \"command\" - interface type | CLI command via script entry | Project pattern from package.json |\n| \"without adding new deps\" | Use only built-in Bun/JS capabilities | Explicit requirement |\n| User types not specified | Developer, CI System, DevOps Engineer | Common YAML validator users |\n| Error detail level | Include line numbers when available | Standard validator behavior |\n| Multi-file support | Single file per invocation | Conservative scope for \"simple\" requirement |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
